{
  "email": "sofia.rossi@brightline.example",
  "reason": "Ticket sender's account, devices, and usual hours for Teams camera and audio issue"
}

{
  "hostname": "BL-LT-0107",
  "reason": "Check whether the user's laptop is online and whether it reports any camera or audio hardware warnings"
}

{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Sofia, sorry about the Teams trouble. Please try these steps first and let us know what happens. 1) In Teams, go to Settings, then Devices, and check that the correct microphone, speaker, and camera are selected. Docks and headsets can add extra devices, so pick the one you are actually using. 2) Check the physical mute key on your laptop and make sure the camera privacy shutter is open. 3) Close other apps that might be using the camera, such as Zoom or browser tabs with video calls. 4) Fully quit Teams (right-click its taskbar icon and choose Quit), then reopen it. 5) Run Teams' 'Make a test call' to check audio. Then reply to this ticket and tell us: whether the camera shows in Teams' device list, whether the problem continues after a restart, and whether you are using your laptop BL-LT-0107 or a different computer. If the camera is not detected by Windows at all, or the problem happens on every call after a restart, we will escalate it to a technician.",
  "internal_note": "Sofia Rossi (user 7, Marketing Coordinator, America/New_York, usual hours 09:00-17:00, account active, no failed logins, not blocked). Reported Teams camera black screen and participants cannot hear her. Only device on record is BL-LT-0107 (Windows 11 Pro, 54.7% disk free), but it shows offline with no check-in for 12 days (last check-in 2026-09-29), so it may not be the device she is using or may be powered off. Followed KB-025 quick checks and fix steps by sending them to the user. Escalation criteria (camera not detected by Windows, or failure on every call after restart) are not yet confirmed. Next: confirm the device in use and the result of the steps. If the camera is missing in Windows, escalate as a hardware fault. The device check-in gap should also be looked at. No actions were queued. KB-012 and KB-006 were not relevant.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-025"
  ],
  "confidence": "medium"
}
